task: cut-refuses-the-document
agent: opus-depth
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/cut-refuses-the-document
worktree: ../okay-wt-cut
spec: specs/codecs.md ("How deep a message may be" — its "what stays asymmetric" paragraph)

why: the operator asked for the rest of it. input-depth-both-wires
  (f9eb4cb1) left ONE difference standing and called it structural: on
  the JSON roads a cut inside a field nobody DECLARED is data nobody
  reads, so the document still decodes, while CBOR refuses by name. I
  justified it by the totality difference. That justification is the
  same SHAPE as the one this arc already found false — "the other wire
  is different" — and a recursive schema can really write such a
  value, so the law "one Schema, one value, one answer on either wire"
  really is broken by it.

  The second thing named in that report was okay-mcp's six
  `case Json.JErr(_) => Nil/None` projections (BACKLOG
  mcp-projection-eats-a-cut). READ BEFORE WRITING, 2026-09-10:
  `Rpc.damaged` already walks the whole tree for a JErr and maps it to
  ParseError -32700, so a cut frame is already an error and those six
  sites are the client's error channel answering correctly. The
  backlog entry was a misread; it gets a test and a deletion, not a
  fix.

what: make the cut a property of the DOCUMENT instead of a spot in it.
  The projection propagates it: a container with a cut child IS the
  cut, so `Json.parse` of a too-deep document answers the cut at the
  root, every decoder's existing JErr refusal catches it wherever it
  sits, and an undeclared deep field refuses exactly as CBOR refuses
  it. That makes the three special cases f9eb4cb1 added (Json.arrived,
  the optional-field guard, Staged.elems + both staged optional
  lookups) UNREACHABLE — they go, and the hot-path `filterNot` comes
  back. `JsonStrict.skipValue` has no tree to propagate through, so it
  bounds its own bracket counter, which is where the strict door's
  undeclared skip refuses.

predict: smaller than what it replaces (one rule in the projection in
  place of four rules in four files), and the only behaviour anyone
  loses is the partial value of a document nested past 256 — which no
  caller can use, because the reader cannot say what it did not
  descend into.
